<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imo Incident</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Imo Mutiny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Imo Incident was a mutiny by unpaid soldiers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> in July 1882. Angered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> rations that were late and spoiled, and by favour shown to a new Japanese-trained unit, they attacked officials and the Japanese legation, and Queen Min fled the palace. Her rival the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Heungseon Daewongun</a> briefly returned to power until Chinese troops restored order and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which tightened its hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imo-incident.jpg" alt="Imo Incident" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 歌川国松 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imogullan1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ganghwa">Treaty of Ganghwa</a> opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, King Gojong's government pursued a policy of enlightenment, taking up Western technology while keeping Korean values. In 1881 Gojong and Queen Min invited the Japanese military attaché Lieutenant Horimoto Reizō to help create a modern army, and a unit of eighty to one hundred young men, the Pyŏlgigun or Special Skills Force, was given Japanese training. In January 1882 the old five-army garrison structure was reorganised into two garrisons.</p><p>Regular soldiers resented the new unit, which was far better equipped and treated. More than 1,000 men had been discharged in the reorganisation, and the rest had not received their pay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> for 13 months. Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>, encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> to sign the treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> concluded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incheon">Incheon</a> on 22 May 1882, hoping to offset Japanese influence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The riot broke out on 23 July 1882 when the soldiers found sand and bad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> in their rations. They wrecked the house of Min Gyeom-ho, whom they suspected of swindling them, seized weapons from an armoury and freed prisoners, and the poor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> joined them. Rioters stabbed Lieutenant Horimoto to death, and a crowd of some 3,000 surrounded the Japanese legation; the minister Hanabusa Yoshitada had it set on fire and escaped with his staff to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incheon">Incheon</a> and then to sea, where the British survey ship HMS Flying Fish picked them up. Six Japanese were killed during the flight.</p><p>On 24 July the rioters forced their way into the royal palace and killed Min Gyeom-ho and about a dozen high officials, among them Heungin-gun Yi Choe-Heung, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Daewongun</a>'s elder brother. They searched for Queen Min, who escaped disguised as a court lady, carried on the back of a guard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> sent Hanabusa back with warships and soldiers, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> sent ships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a> and about 4,500 troops under General Wu Changqing. After seizing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Daewongun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> had him confined for three years near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. China then stationed troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, sent advisers including Paul Georg von Möllendorff, set up a Korean Maritime Customs Service in 1883 and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> train new Korean units. In Japan the crisis convinced leaders to expand the armed forces, and rivalry over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> led on to the failed coup of 1884 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> of 1894.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Imo Incident” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Joseon became a battleground for Chinese, Japanese and Russian influence: the <b>Imo Incident</b> of 1882 and the Gapsin Coup of 1884 brought Chinese and Japanese troops into Seoul.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
